// scrml.dev — landing page (/)
//
// Tone: honest current-state. User-ratified at S98B per the architecture
// DD §8.3 question. Matches Rule 1 + user's S95 voice on adoption
// framing ("zero adoption is a blessing in disguise right now").
//
// What this page does NOT do: marketing-flagship "three big claims with
// worked examples." That's Option (a). The user picked (b) — honest
// current-state. Read the Articles section for the why behind any claim.
//
// CURRENCY AUDIT 2026-08-02. This page had gone 4 minor versions stale: it
// described v0.3.0 as the newest cut while the header pill read v0.7.1, and
// it claimed the reference was "day-1" when it is 73/73 written, 0 stubs.
// Every claim below was re-verified against ../scrml (changelog + SPEC +
// tree) on that date.
//
// CURRENCY PASS 2026-09-29 (v0.8.0; auth claim corrected per SPEC §34
// W-AUTH-CONTENT-NOT-GATED; pending list rewritten).
//
// RULE FOR EDITORS — prefer claims that cannot rot. "v0.8.0 was tagged on
// 2026-09-26" stays true forever; "we're at day-1" was false within a week.
// Do not reintroduce day-N targets, "as of today", or a version peg that
// has to be hand-bumped to stay honest.

<page>
    <title>scrml — a full-stack language for the browser</title>

    <article class="prose prose-slate max-w-3xl">

        <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight text-slate-900">
            scrml is a language in active development.
        </h1>

        <p class="text-xl text-slate-700 mt-6 leading-relaxed">
            A full-stack language for the browser. Multi-page apps,
            real-time channels, SQL-as-syntax, state machines as
            first-class. One file format. The compiler owns the wiring.
        </p>

        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-slate-900 mt-12 mb-4">
            What works today
        </h2>

        <ul class="space-y-2 text-slate-700">
            <li>
                <strong>v0.8.0</strong> is the current release, tagged
                2026-09-26. It is the stable floor of the working compiler
                (written in TypeScript) and carries three months of fixes and
                additions on top of v0.7.1 &mdash; the full list is in the
                <a href="https://github.com/bryanmaclee/scrml/blob/main/docs/changelog.md"
                   class="text-blue-700 hover:text-blue-900 underline">changelog</a>.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>v0.4.0 &ndash; v0.6.0</strong> added generation from
                types &mdash; <code>&lt;formFor for=StructType/&gt;</code>
                builds a form, its state cell, its validity surface and its
                submit path; <code>schemaFor(StructType)</code> inside a
                <code>&lt;schema&gt;</code> builds the SQL DDL, and enum fields
                carry their variants across the database boundary. The same
                arc expanded the built-in Tailwind surface.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>v0.3.0</strong> shipped content-addressed output and
                <code>&lt;auth role&gt;</code> gates. Be precise about what
                <code>&lt;auth&gt;</code> does: it decides which client code
                mounts for which role. It is not content secrecy &mdash; the
                gated HTML is still sent to every viewer, so anything secret
                belongs on the server. Per-role JavaScript chunks exist but are
                opt-in (<code>scrml compile --emit-per-route</code>).
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>v0.2.x</strong> laid the foundational reactive
                surface: V5-strict state, validators, the auto-synthesized
                validity surface, and the pattern-matching ladder.
            </li>
            <li>
                Engines are the UI centerpiece. Markup is a first-class
                value type (a stated pillar of the spec, not a library
                trick). The server boundary disappears.
            </li>
            <li>
                The reference in these docs is <strong>fully written</strong>
                &mdash; 73 of 73 pages across elements, keywords, contexts
                and error codes, with no stubs.
            </li>
        </ul>

        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-slate-900 mt-12 mb-4">
            What's pending
        </h2>

        <ul class="space-y-2 text-slate-700">
            <li>
                A second compiler, written in scrml itself, is being built as a
                bootstrap in the compiler repo (<code>compiler/self-host-v2/</code>).
                Its first slices parse, analyze and lower real programs. It is
                not yet a compiler you can use.
            </li>
            <li>
                The next language model &mdash; declarations, instances and
                value contracts (SPEC &sect;66) &mdash; is written into the
                specification but is <strong>not</strong> in the shipping
                compiler. The bootstrap compiler is where it is being built.
            </li>
            <li>
                Several decided changes are not built yet: conditions that must
                be a boolean or a presence test (no JavaScript-style
                truthiness), a <code>decimal</code> number type, and arithmetic
                operators restricted to numbers. Until they ship, the compiler
                behaves as it does today.
            </li>
            <li>
                Depth, not coverage, in these docs. Every reference page is
                written; not every one of them is yet written to the depth
                MDN sets as the bar.
            </li>
        </ul>

        <div class="mt-12 p-6 border border-slate-200 rounded-lg bg-slate-50">
            <h3 class="font-semibold text-slate-900 mb-2">
                Three pages that show the shape of the language:
            </h3>
            <ul class="space-y-1">
                <li>
                    <a hard href="/reference/elements/engine"
                       class="text-blue-700 hover:text-blue-900 underline">
                        &lt;engine&gt; — state machines as a first-class element
                    </a>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <a hard href="/reference/elements/errors"
                       class="text-blue-700 hover:text-blue-900 underline">
                        &lt;errors of=…/&gt; — the auto-synthesized validity surface
                    </a>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <a hard href="/reference/contexts/sql"
                       class="text-blue-700 hover:text-blue-900 underline">
                        ?{ } — SQL as syntax; the server boundary disappears
                    </a>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
            <a hard href="/getting-started"
               class="px-5 py-2.5 bg-slate-900 text-white rounded-md font-medium hover:bg-slate-800">
                New here? &rarr; Get started
            </a>
            <a hard href="/reference"
               class="px-5 py-2.5 border border-slate-300 text-slate-900 rounded-md font-medium hover:bg-slate-50">
                Ready to dig in &rarr; Reference
            </a>
            <a hard href="/articles"
               class="px-5 py-2.5 border border-slate-300 text-slate-900 rounded-md font-medium hover:bg-slate-50">
                Want the why? &rarr; Articles
            </a>
        </div>

        <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-slate-900 mt-16 mb-4">
            Adoption framing — honest
        </h2>

        <p class="text-slate-700 leading-relaxed">
            scrml has near-zero adoption right now. That's a known state,
            not a problem we're papering over. LLMs have broken the
            historical friction-tipping dynamic in language adoption:
            developers who would have switched stacks in 2018 due to
            framework friction now use the same stacks with LLM assistance
            and tolerate the rough edges. The motivation to adopt a new
            language has waned.
        </p>

        <p class="text-slate-700 leading-relaxed mt-4">
            scrml's bet is that it makes LLMs more efficient too — the
            same compiler-owned wiring that the developer doesn't have to
            think about is wiring the LLM doesn't have to think about.
            But until the language is serious and complete enough to be
            un-ignorable, adoption is not the expected outcome of this
            decade's pattern. We're building anyway.
        </p>

        <p class="text-slate-700 leading-relaxed mt-4">
            If that framing resonates &mdash; build something with it. If
            it doesn't &mdash; the existing stacks are fine and we don't
            begrudge anyone who stays put.
        </p>

    </article>

</page>
